# FOR loop whose final value is modified inside the loop body.
#
# What the standard says: ed.3 7.3.3.4.2: the FOR statement is for loops
# whose number of iterations can be determined in advance; the control
# variable, initial value, final value and increment "shall not be altered
# by any of the repeated statements". Modifying the final value is
# therefore a violation and the behaviour is not defined: a runtime may
# evaluate the final value once (3 iterations for start_n = 3) or on every
# iteration (unbounded, here stopped by the count >= 50 guard).
#
# What IronPLC 0.251.0 (5ea0610) does: the final value is re-read on
# every iteration, so the loop only stops at the guard: count = 50 and n
# ended at start_n + 50. Recorded 2026-10-31.
period_ms: 100
program: main
inputs: start_n
outputs: count n_after
status: implementation-defined
reference: IEC 61131-3 ed.3 7.3.3.4.2 (FOR)
---
0 start_n=3 => count=50 n_after=53
# initial value 1 > final value 0: zero iterations whatever the rule
1 start_n=0 => count=0 n_after=0
2 start_n=10 => count=50 n_after=60
